{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>Let us make adam</b>. Some say that the word נעשה is a noun of the nifal binyan, like \"and what was prepared for one day\" (Nehemiah 5:18), and they say that \"in our image, after our likeness\" are the words of Moses, and he explained \"and Elohim created the adam in His image\" — that the vav returns to the adam, and they interpreted \"in the image of Elohim\" (Genesis 9:6) — that Elohim is joined with עשה, as though saying that Elohim made the adam in an image. And this interpretation is lacking sense, for the first [verse] ought to have been so, and he should have said, 'And Elohim said, let an adam be made,' and thus He made him in His image. Moreover, how does the vav in בצלמו return to the adam? Behold, he has an image before he exists? And what reason is there in \"whoever sheds the blood of the adam, by the adam shall his blood be shed\" (Genesis 9:6), because the adam was created in the image? Every soul too would have had an image. And the Gaon [Saadia] said that the meaning of \"in our image, after our likeness\" concerns dominion, and its sense is \"in an image\" that He saw in wisdom, for it is good, and on account of the honor of the adam they attached it to Elohim — as in, \"and from his land they came forth\" (Ezekiel 36:20), for \"to YHWH is the earth and its fullness.\" And he said regarding the word נעשה — if it is plural, so is the custom of kings to speak, and like it, \"ונתנה לך also this\" (Genesis 28:27 [sic]), \"נאמר קדם מלכא\" (Daniel 2:36), \"perhaps I will be able נכה him\" (Numbers 22:6). And these witnesses are false witnesses, for \"ונתנה לך\" is nifal binyan, and like it \"and the city נתנה\" (Jeremiah 32:24), and the vav they converted to future tense as is the rule of every past verb, and the meaning is ותינתן לך. \"ונכה בו\" is ומחנהו, or it may be a noun of the agent, like \"ונקה לא אנקך\" (Jeremiah 46:28), even though we find \"לא נכו\" (Exodus 9:32), for a binyan whose active noun is not attested does not occur, except from the root כבד. And R. Moses the Kohen the Sephardi erred in his book. And the word \"נאמר קדם מלכא\" (Daniel 2:36) is Aramaic — how would Daniel speak in a grandiose tongue with Nebuchadnezzar, who was king of kings? And now I will explain. Know that all the work of Creation was created for the honor of the adam, by the command of HaShem. And the plants — the earth and the waters brought them forth, and all the souls of living things. And afterward HaShem said to the angels, 'Let us make adam' — we will deal with him, and not the waters and the earth. And since we know that the Torah spoke in the language of human beings — for the speaker is human and so is the listener, and no person can speak of things above him or below him, except by way of the likeness of the adam. And thus it said, \"the mouth of the earth\" (Numbers 13:29 [sic]), \"the hand of the Jordan,\" and \"the chief of the dust of the world\" (Proverbs 8:26) — and far be it, far be it that there be a likeness to HaShem, as it said, \"To whom will you liken Me?\" (Isaiah 40:25). And because the supernal soul of the adam, which does not die, is likened in its being to HaShem, and is not a body, and fills all of him, and the body of the adam is like a microcosm — blessed be HaShem, who began with the great and completed with the small. And the prophet too said he saw the glory of Elohim like the appearance of an adam. And HaShem is the One, and He formed everything, and He is everything — and I cannot explain. And the adam was created at first with two faces: and behold he is one, and also she is two. And behold, \"in the image of Elohim\" — an angel. And he was created male and female. And the word \"be fruitful and multiply\" in the case of the adam is a blessing, like in the creation of the waters; only it is a commandment, which our ancestors of blessed memory transmitted. And He placed this verse as a remembrance of the matter."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra cites Genesis 28:27 for 'ונתנה לך' but the verse is 31:27; the Hebrew as printed reads 'כח' and 'כו' in the citations of Numbers 13:29 and Proverbs 8:26, rendered literally. The final sentences contain dense sod-type hints rendered without expansion per discipline."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:42:55.667315111Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}